"""Uniform public E2 research specification; never contains instance truth."""

PUBLIC_RESEARCH_CONTRACT = "Public research contract and data legend\n\nAudit the supplied research script against the rules below. Keep its research equations, three candidate strategies, functional outputs and numerical conventions; repair violations of the stated information, identity, partition, execution and inference rules. Submit findings at actual source lines and a complete general patched script that accepts the same research-data JSON object on different data (the data object within the solver input, without the surrounding research_source/contract fields). The script is the object being audited and may violate these rules; its current expressions are not authoritative overrides of this contract.\n\nData and units\n\nAll data are synthetic. Time indices are integer sessions, zero based. Asset indices identify stable listings and index the columns of all time-by-asset arrays. For T sessions and A listings, returns, prices and active have shape T by A. Returns are decimal simple session returns; prices are positive synthetic price units. Returns for inactive listings are represented as zero. prices[0] is 100 for each listing; for t>=1, prices[t][a] = prices[t-1][a]*(1+returns[t][a]). Do not reinterpret zero inactive returns as evidence that a listing is eligible. The supplied price/return construction, including inactive rows, is part of the task and must not be replaced.\n\nlisting_ids has length A and gives stable listing identity for each column. active[t][a] is membership/activity as of session t; use that session's activity when forming its signal. Future final-session activity must not determine historical membership. identifier_changes has length A and records the session of an identifier change, with a negative value for no change. A changed display identifier does not change stable listing identity or its historical column. ticker_owner is a length-A current-identifier-join mapping supplied as a potentially misleading alternative to stable identity. In ticker_owner, -1 denotes a missing current-identifier join and must never select the last listing; negative missing-join values are not valid Python-negative-index fallbacks to another listing. Historical features and fundamentals for listing a use stable column/list index a, not a substitute obtained from the current identifier mapping.\n\nfundamentals is a length-A list; fundamentals[a] is an ordered list of records [period_end, available_day, value]. The first two entries are integer session indices; value is the initial_value, a synthetic fundamental value in arbitrary units. A record is usable for signal session t only when available_day <= t. period_end describes the accounting period and does not establish information availability. Use the last eligible record in the supplied record order; if none is available, the fundamental contribution is zero. Do not use a later record merely because its period ended earlier.\n\nbounds = [train_start, train_end, select_end, eval_end]. Training is [train_start, train_end), strategy selection is [train_end, select_end), and evaluation is [select_end, eval_end), all half open. horizon is the positive integer forward-label horizon in sessions. Full-history arrays include prehistory and future records for legitimate label construction and reporting; their presence does not authorize future information in decisions.\n\nEstimation and labels\n\nFor each listing a, fit the feature center on returns[t][a] for train_start <= t < train_end. Fit its scale on that same training interval as the population root mean square deviation from that center, floored at 1e-8. Neither transform may use selection, evaluation or later observations.\n\nThere are exactly three candidate strategies, ordered by lag 1, 3 and 7. For strategy lag L, the training feature at timestamp t for listing a is (returns[t-L][a]-center[a])/scale[a]. Its forward training label is the sum of returns[u][a] over t+1 <= u <= t+horizon. Fit only timestamps train_start+7 <= t < train_end-horizon, so every label satisfies t+horizon < train_end and is fully inside training. The coefficient is the sum of feature*label over these timestamps and all listings, divided by 1e-8 plus the sum of feature squared over the same observations. Preserve this equation and its numerical conventions; do not add a different regression or retune strategy parameters.\n\nSignal, orders and fills\n\nFinalize the signal at close t, using only information available by t. For each strategy and listing a, its raw exposure is coefficient*(returns[t-L][a]-center[a])/scale[a] + 0.01*fundamental_value_asof_t. Apply tanh(10*raw) for an active listing at t, otherwise zero. Divide all exposures for that strategy/timestamp by max(1, sum of their absolute values). These normalized exposures are the signal/order weights for timestamp t.\n\nThe order from signal t must fill at close t+1, using prices[t+1], and realizes its one-session portfolio return at close t+2. Its return is sum over listings a of weights_t[a]*(prices[t+2][a]/prices[t+1][a]-1). A signal from t+1 must not replace the finalized signal t for this order. A price at submission t must not replace its next-close fill. No later price, return or unavailable fundamental may affect an earlier signal or fully realized earlier result.\n\nConstruct strategy order paths for train_end <= t < eval_end. Turnover at order t is the sum of absolute weight changes from the preceding order weights in this constructed path; weights before its first order are the zero vector. Preserve this initialization when reporting later evaluation turnover.\n\nSelection and inference\n\nChoose a single strategy by maximum sum of its selection-order returns for train_end <= t < select_end-2. These are exactly the selection orders whose realization < select_end, equivalently t+2 < select_end. Break ties by the earliest candidate index, as Python max over candidate indices in their declared order does. Do not choose a strategy using evaluation or later returns, or an unrealized boundary order.\n\nReport evaluation orders for select_end <= t < eval_end. selected_strategy is the chosen candidate's zero-based index in lag order [1,3,7]. Its evaluation return path is the selected candidate path. Starting from wealth 1, equity successively multiplies by 1+each evaluation return. net_mean is their arithmetic mean. net_std is their population standard deviation floored at 1e-12. Compute the existing two-sided normal approximation erfc(abs(net_mean)/net_std*sqrt(number_of_evaluation_orders)/sqrt(2)), and apply the Bonferroni adjustment for three trials: adjusted_p = min(1, this value*3). All three declared candidate trials count, regardless of which one is selected. Preserve these formulas and do not substitute a different significance test or economic model.\n\nRequired complete output\n\nEmit exactly the functional JSON object used by the supplied research script, with finite numeric values and the following fields. Let E = eval_end-select_end. holdings, fill_prices, strategy_returns and turnover are each length-3 lists, ordered by lag [1,3,7]. holdings and fill_prices each contain E rows of length A per candidate; strategy_returns and turnover each contain E scalar values per candidate. fill_prices are the required next-close prices for each reported order. returns and equity each have E scalar values for the selected strategy. selected_strategy is its integer index. net_mean, net_std and adjusted_p are scalar values defined above. Full paths and nontrivial functionality are required; summaries, constants or disabled calculations do not substitute for them.\n\ntraining_label_mean is a legitimate descriptive report: average the horizon-session forward labels for listing index 0 over train_start <= t < train_end-horizon. This report includes all purged training-label timestamps in that range, while coefficient fitting begins at train_start+7 as stated separately. descriptive_mean is the arithmetic mean of returns[t][0] over every supplied session 0 <= t < T. These reports do not feed fitted transforms, coefficients, signal formation or strategy selection. Forward references used only in the purged training labels and full-history descriptive reporting are legitimate. label_report and moment_report are optional Boolean presentation flags selecting equivalent implementations of these respective report calculations; changing a flag must not change either report's numerical value or any other required output.\n\nOutput row t for holdings/returns denotes the order generated by signal t, with the fill and realization timing above. Evaluation ends at its last order timestamp, not at its last realization; supplied price rows support those final realizations. Preserve all output shapes, ordering, identities, equations and report values on new data. Finding correctness alone cannot establish that a patch satisfies this contract.\n"
